OL,3,  V-,,%-,,%-,,%S#"!##!###!##!
OL,5, The hijacked Kuwaiti airliner in Iran
OL,6, has been refuelled and is apparently
OL,7, preparing to leave Mashad airport.
OL,9,FThe move follows the firing of warning
OL,10,Fshots by the hijackers at security
OL,11,Fforces on the tarmac and a demand that
OL,12,Fobstacles on the runway be removed.
OL,14,FThe release of 17 prisoners held in
OL,15,FKuwaiti jails for terrorist offences
OL,16,Fis the main demand of the hijackers.
OL,18,FAll British hostages have been released
OL,19,Fbut about 50 people, mostly Kuwaitis,
OL,20,Fare still on board.
OL,23,T]Cfrom 07-04-88 / teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,182,190,120,250,0,100
PN,18103
SC,0003
CT,8,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F181A1775577779
OL,1,  V<,,4<,,4<,,4Spp0pp0p``0pp0      G3/12
OL,2,T]V5b(55b(55j5Sj5.%jj5/l4
OL,3,  V-,,%-,,%-,,%S#"!##!###!##!
OL,5, The Kuwaiti government has sent an
OL,6, aircraft to Mashad in Iran to collect
OL,7, the 22 Britons and other passengers
OL,8, freed from the hijacked airliner.
OL,10,FThe freed Britons are expected to leave
OL,11,FKuwait for Britain soon after arriving
OL,12,Ffrom Mashad.
OL,14,FA British diplomat, Mr Paul Ramsay, is
OL,15,Fflying to Tehran tonight to help in the
OL,16,Foperation to bring home the freed
OL,17,FBritons.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FThe Foreign Office said they were all
OL,20,Ffit and well.
OL,21,F
OL,23,T]Cfrom 07-04-88 / teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
